✅ 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐈𝐧𝐭𝐞𝐫𝐯𝐢𝐞𝐰 𝐒𝐜𝐞𝐧𝐚𝐫𝐢𝐨 #8 – 𝑹𝒖𝒏 𝑺𝒑𝒆𝒄𝒊𝒇𝒊𝒄 𝑻𝒂𝒔𝒌𝒔 𝒘𝒊𝒕𝒉 𝑻𝒂𝒈𝒔 𝒊𝒏 𝑨𝒏𝒔𝒊𝒃𝒍𝒆

🧠 𝐐𝐮𝐞𝐬𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧:

How do you run only selected tasks from a large Ansible playbook without executing everything?

💡 𝐑𝐞𝐚𝐥-𝐖𝐨𝐫𝐥𝐝 𝐔𝐬𝐞 𝐂𝐚𝐬𝐞:

Imagine you have a big playbook that installs the app, sets up the DB, configures Nginx, and applies monitoring.

But today, you just need to 𝒓𝒆𝒔𝒕𝒂𝒓𝒕 𝒕𝒉𝒆 𝒅𝒂𝒕𝒂𝒃𝒂𝒔𝒆, 𝒏𝒐 𝒏𝒆𝒆𝒅 𝒕𝒐 𝒕𝒐𝒖𝒄𝒉 𝒕𝒉𝒆 𝒓𝒆𝒔𝒕.

That’s where tags come in.

🗂️ Think of tags like bookmarks — they help you jump straight to what matters, skipping everything else.

💡 𝐏𝐫𝐨 𝐓𝐢𝐩𝐬:

✅ Add 𝒍𝒐𝒈𝒊𝒄𝒂𝒍 𝒕𝒂𝒈𝒔 to every task (e.g., db, web, monitoring)
✅ Combine tags with --𝒍𝒊𝒎𝒊𝒕 for even more precise control
✅ Use --𝒔𝒌𝒊𝒑-𝒕𝒂𝒈𝒔 when you want to run everything except a certain task group

➡️ To run only DB tasks:

▶ 𝐇𝐢𝐠𝐡𝐥𝐢𝐠𝐡𝐭𝐞𝐝 𝐂𝐨𝐦𝐦𝐚𝐧𝐝:

𝒂𝒏𝒔𝒊𝒃𝒍𝒆-𝒑𝒍𝒂𝒚𝒃𝒐𝒐𝒌 𝒔𝒊𝒕𝒆.𝒚𝒎𝒍 --𝒕𝒂𝒈𝒔 "𝒅𝒃"

🔺 Run this to apply only DB-related tasks



=================
site.yaml
---
- name: Scenario #8 - Use Tags to Run Specific Tasks
  hosts: all
  become: yes

  tasks:
    - name: Install PostgreSQL
      apt:
        name: postgresql
        state: present
      tags: db

    - name: Start PostgreSQL service
      service:
        name: postgresql
        state: started
      tags: db

